bility<br /> <br /> Aside these, you are completly right, in a perfect world, especially in the 21st century vampires would have their own little stock of humans not risking the hunt<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1515091-drama-or-realism?p=1515097#post1515097\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Theoretically all vampires are descended from Caine, and his civilization. Yet its not like there is a vampire language which every vampire has to learn once turned.</div> </div> </div> </div>I beg to differ<br /> Vampirism is not a civilization, but a condition<br /> Every vampire - well except the Nictuku who mostly were monsters to begin with and the Abominations and the Kyasid/Maeghar - were Human and came from the human civlization and cultural background<br /> It is hardly fair to expect unity from the Vampires when Humanity (or any other race in WoD) faild to mastering that...<br /> The closest is the Salubri (well the original idea at leats of them) with the requirement to know Enochian, the Language of the First City - the bit bastarized version of the First Tounge of the Fallen, but the closest to that literally unifiyng language wich is so primodal and strong a Human once hear it, all the other &quot;lesser&quot; languages are basically deleted from its brain\u200b\u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","name":"Shadeprowler","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23890_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"928"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-15T06:19:23-07:00","id":1515106,"title":"","content":"I like the idea of vampires weeping tears of blood, but I do draw the line at other bodily fluids.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Amethyst is my birthstone. She/they.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26996-amethyst","name":"Amethyst","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"873"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-15T08:42:42-07:00","id":1515133,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadeprowler</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1515091-drama-or-realism?p=1515105#post1515105\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I beg to differ<br /> Vampirism is not a civilization, but a condition<br /> Every vampire - well except the Nictuku who mostly were monsters to begin with and the Abominations and the Kyasid/Maeghar - were Human and came from the human civlization and cultural background<br /> It is hardly fair to expect unity from the Vampires when Humanity (or any other race in WoD) faild to mastering that...<br /> The closest is the Salubri (well the original idea at leats of them) with the requirement to know Enochian, the Language of the First City - the bit bastarized version of the First Tounge of the Fallen, but the closest to that literally unifiyng language wich is so primodal and strong a Human once hear it, all the other &quot;lesser&quot; languages are basically deleted from its brain\u200b\u200b</div> </div> </div> </div>Being a cainite vampire is being part of a social group with its own needs and requirements. Seeing that there isn't a natural lifespan for vampires that means that time doesn't automatically change a groups culture due to the vanguard dying off due to old age.<br /> <br /> Yes, new vampires are embraced from different time periods and cultures but they are being inducted into a far older culture. Remember the vampire population generally remains in ratio with the human population, thus it is only in the recent times that the vampire population reached the highest numbers of being 80,000 strong. Before 1800 the vampire population should only have been around 10,000. That is incredibly small, and due to the fact that most clans/bloodlines were dependent on human cities, they stayed remarkably local. How could a vampire culture, if only on a club house level not develop?<br /> <br /> Also the Kindred of the East game line started going in this direction, with the Kingdom of Mu, the proto language of kaja, the idea that each Kuei-jin dharma had its own cultural norms and ideas. KotE also had asian vampirism be a hereditary issue as much as a spiritual one. Being that KotE was so late in the development cycle, you can see where the developers were already designing around certain things from the earlier games. Then again KotE also ties WoD to the Exalted setting so take all of that with a grain of salt.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-15T09:38:35-07:00","id":1515142,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1515091-drama-or-realism?p=1515133#post1515133\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Before 1800 the vampire population should only have been around 10,000. .</div> </div> </div> </div>From one hand, yes it is true<br /> But on the other, the Feast of Folly (no official date was given, but It must happened during the late 8th century. Several clues around to pinpoint the date: The embrace of Khurshid, he spent a century manipulating the Nosferatu, and his embrace was in late 7th century. And he was locked up; It was after Apacia went to torpor (which was during mid 7th centuy) Derinkuyu was used by humans in the Arab-Byzantine war starting from 780, so it had to be before that date and it was before the Embrace of Augustus Giovanni, who was embraced in 1005. Players guide to the High Clans had something that narrows things down a bit more. It mentions when Cappadocious awakened, the caravan had travellers from Baghdad that he fed off of. That gives us a time after 762CE since the founding of Baghdad) happened because Cappadocius looked up from his books to hear the complaints of the humans who lived with Cappadocians in Kaymakli. There, he found that the prodigious breeding of his childer had led to a population crisis: anemic humans, disease spread by the constant presence of corpses, and a society on the brink. Realizing that something had to be done, Cappadocius returned to Erciyes and perused the Book of Nod. Then, one night, he called all Cappadocians to join him in Kaymakli, <b>resulting in a pilgrimage of over 12,000 Cainites </b>to a tiny Turkish underground city. - and many Cappadocian didn't make it or outright refused to go.<br /> Kind of a huge number<br /> The Tzimisce similarly was a populous Clan, their whole culture was centered around the web of children, grandchildren and great grand children ruling over a given domain.<br /> Cainite were numerous in the antiquity if we look them Caln by Clan<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1515091-drama-or-realism?p=1515133#post1515133\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Also the Kindred of the East game line started going in this direction</div> </div> </div> </div>Also true<br /> My point is, with every new generation of humans came a new generation (not the Generation sense, but as embraced from that generation) of Cainite fledgelings bringing with them their own knowledge and belief systems and this trend repeted itself to this day; many Cainite Embrace in the depserate attempt to have soemone who can help them to keep in touch with the everchanging world<br /> Clan Culture and customs developed, sure, but not a Cainite civilization; vampires remained in a sense, Humans, constantly updating themselves - aside from a few who refused to do it accepting the disadvantages it may mean.<br /> Anastazi Ruthven is a good example, he is 500+ years old, yet He's also become Hunedoara castle's resident fix-it man and token technology buff. He owns a gas generator which he uses to power his quarters\u2019 electric lights. He hasn't acquired a computer because they still intimidate him, but he has a number of cheap electronic handheld games from the 80's and early 90's littering one of his tables and a portable stereo with a small CD collection, as well as myriad other gadgets that were cutting-edge at the time he bought them.<br /> But the part, he hasn't acquired a computer because they still intimidate him is some of the most human thing I ever heard in regard of a vampire<br /> My grandpa refused even go near to a phone... and considered lobsters &quot;big red bugs&quot;<br /> And he wasn't even a vampire<br /> I think...<br /> My father on the other hand basically know more about a computer than me, frighteningly more to be honest<br /> <br /> \u200b\u200b<br /> \u200b<br /> \u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","name":"Shadeprowler","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23890_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"928"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-15T09:56:12-07:00","id":1515144,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1515091-drama-or-realism?p=1515097#post1515097\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Even the idea of a city being home to multiple clans/bloodlines is counter intuitive because that just creates infighting and power struggles. If each city was controlled by a given clan exclusively then the power structure would be far more functional. This becomes very important when the idea that shifters are constantly circling the cities trying to keep the vampires contained and occasionally doing raids in an attempt to purge the vampires. Whose is going to be a Harpy when you have guard duty to keep an eye out for a shifter raid?</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't think it's that strange, because if you stop to think about it, the camarilla clans came together because they saw value in the strength of other clans and if you look at it well then they do it more as a way to use themselves and if you think about each clan it has a well-established role in the camarilla at least, on the sabbat it is more difficult.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Perhaps most egregious of all is the vampire who is more than 50 years old and yet still behaves like a teenager or cartoon villain. Anyone who is that stupid won't survive long in the world of darkness setting. If just a couple years of war changes a person into a hardened survivor, how can a vampire who has survived for centuries place any importance on things as useless as fashion or courtly intrigues of who is sleeping with who? To a certain degree this issue was fixed in Kindred of the East but somehow even with new editions of VtM, centuries old vampires are still behaving like the Lost Boys coterie. </div> </div> </div>I read in the book of the Giovanni clan that it often happens that an elder treats a neonate as a son and this generates kindreds that look like grown up and spoiled children, I imagine that the circumstances may be similar here, or it could even be a disguise, or maybe the Fifty-year-old child can be a useful pawn after all<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Lastly there is a complete disconnect between the mechanics of the embrace and the affect it would have on the vampire population demographics. If the setting of WoD is as cut throat and lethal as it is made out to be, then when given the choice between embracing a hyper competent mortal or a random mortal who is hot, no one could waste the resources or time to embrace and raise a neonate who didn't already carry their own weight but give a benefit in addition to that.<br /> <br /> When given the choice between a special forces soldier, a top tier scientist, a fortune 500 company owner, or a random highschooler who lacks experience in anything, why would you ever choose the highschooler except for contrived story reasons or for player options? </div> </div> </div>Possible puppet, accidental embrace, spur of the moment, punishment or even use as a shovel head. Or kindred just wants a pet anyway.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27832-psicose","name":"Psicose","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27832_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"892"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-15T11:02:01-07:00","id":1515153,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1515091-drama-or-realism?p=1515133#post1515133\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">. Remember the vampire population generally remains in ratio with the human population, thus it is only in the recent times that the vampire population reached the highest numbers of being 80,000 strong. Before 1800 the vampire population should only have been around 10,000. That is incredibly small, and due to the fact that most clans/bloodlines were dependent on human cities, they stayed remarkably local. How could a vampire culture, if only on a club house level not develop?<br /> .</div> </div> </div> </div>I have really strong doubts about that. Vampire numbers have always been limited by two factors<br /> What the masquerade can take. <br /> How many the prince/rulling elders think they can handle<br /> <br /> Like, without an external threat, A vampire could survive with 60 humans in a village no problem so long as he doesn't spend blood for anything other than rising. Give him twenty more and he'll be substantially better off. <br /> <br /> Citys are a little more complicated because unless everything's very carefully planned some demographics will be disproportionately targeted, but a medieval city of 5000 could happily support 20 vampires I think.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-17T09:56:28-07:00","id":1515387,"title":"","content":"I do find it hard to balance drama and realism given the game we are playing, but one hard rule is that neonates with a few decades under there belt are becoming hard edged survivors and are starting to think of ways to make there existence more comfortable and becoming more pragmatic with whatever ideals they had as mortals or fresh faced neonates. Mick Farren series of vampire novels were good at showing how much even a few decades would change a former mortal mindset let alone centuries.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Power that needs to advertise itself is already losing&quot; Grand Admiral Thrawn <br /> <br /> &quot;Coronation Starscream? This is bad comedy!&quot; Galvatron","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","name":"Lysander","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27361_11.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-17T14:38:09-07:00","id":1515418,"title":"","content":"You only need enough &quot;realism&quot; to help your game table maintain their suspension of disbelief. People don't question the basic assumptions of any setting they play in (in a supernatural horror setting like the World of Darkness, people know that magic and the supernatural are &quot;real&quot;). Because magic isn't real, there is some arbitrariness of what works in the setting. But the sourcebooks generally lay out the mechanics, and players therefore accept that is how it is in this setting or that one, provided it does not lead to outlandish conclusions that break their immersion.<br /> <br /> There is also an issue of the age demographic of the genre. Something written for children or adolescents does not need the same level of realism as writing aimed for an adult audience. Problems there only happen when the audience of the age demographic grows up, yet expects the genre to grow up with them instead of remaining where it was. This is a big problems today for the American comic book superhero genre - something originally aimed at 12 year olds - with all the sophistication required to satisfy the expectations of a 12 year old - is now expected to be &quot;real&quot; for an audience that expects something more appropriate for an adult audience. It undermines the setting though since those worlds were originally written for children. It's not that you cannot explore a world where people somehow get super powers and target it to an adult audience. It's just that it will not be the core Marvel and DC settings, just like Sgt Rock or The Howling Commandos will not be the same as The Thin Red Line or All Quiet on the Western Front.<br /> <br /> There are some players, GMs, and sourcebook writers who really like to detail things past the usual need of the gaming table. This is generally stuff that is mostly flavor text. When they do, they can cause issues because other players, GMs, writers, etc. have different visions of the same fictional concepts. One is not any more &quot;realistic&quot; than the other. It is just a preference, a taste, a subjective opinion.<br /> <br /> Usually by &quot;realism&quot;, many people mean that the characters behave &quot;realistically&quot; given the setting. When established characters suddenly stop being portrayed they were before, it gets confusing. When characters make decisions based on the plot needs of the story writer rather than how they've been portrayed, that isn't &quot;realistic&quot;.<br /> <br /> The other point is when something new gets introduced to a setting that fundamentally alters it in some way, dictating major changes yet the setting and the people in it don't change to reflect that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-18T00:58:06-07:00","id":1515447,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1515091-drama-or-realism?p=1515097#post1515097\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Even the idea of a city being home to multiple clans/bloodlines is counter intuitive because that just creates infighting and power struggles. If each city was controlled by a given clan exclusively then the power structure would be far more functional.</div> </div> </div> </div>Curiously enough, the original setting <i>was</i> one where a city being home to multiple clans was a very recent phenomenon. This detail is largely forgotten these days, for several reasons. Perhaps chief among them is that it directly contradicts the entire <i>Dark Ages</i> game line. The early core books had the following to say about historical vampire populations (emphases mine; note that when the text talks about &quot;hunters,&quot; it means vampires as predators, not mortal vampire hunters):<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vampire: The Masquerade 2nd Edition, page 31</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Only so many hunters can be supported by one herd. In years past <b>only one Kindred lived within each city</b> and each could claim it as her own domain. When cities were small, being <b>a race of loners</b> was an effective survival tactic. However, as cities have grown, so has the population of hunters. Now it is no longer rare for many to live within the same domain.</div> </div> </div> </div>Personally, I like this historical background for the modern-nights game more than I like the &quot;vampire society is eternal and static&quot; model that the later editions have adopted. The original game proposed a setting where Kindred culture had gone through a seismic (and destabilizing) shift in recent history, roughly corresponding with the Industrial Revolution in the mortal world. This has vast implications for many core tenets of the game. Does it even make sense to talk about &quot;clans&quot; as meaningful entities before the modern period? Perhaps the idea of vampires identifying with &quot;clans&quot; is as recent as the Camarilla and Sabbat themselves.<br /> <br /> However, like I suggested above, adopting this historical background for the game kind of makes it necessary to... give up on the <i>Dark Ages</i> game line as a whole. The published version of the medieval setting simply falls apart if you accept the premise that all vampires across the globe used to be solitary predators, and each city was the domain of just one vampire (and maybe his or her immediate childer). I'm not sure if it's possible to construct a playable setting out of that premise, but it's interesting to think about.\u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1190-elphilm","name":"Elphilm","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1190_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"763"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-18T12:48:30-07:00","id":1515470,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Elphilm</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1515091-drama-or-realism?p=1515447#post1515447\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Perhaps chief among them is that it directly contradicts the entire <i>Dark Ages</i> game line.</div> </div> </div> </div>Absolutely. While I like the original Vampire: the Dark Ages corebook and setting for a lot of reasons, there was really insufficient thinking of how the different historical era should impact the setting. Other than removing the sects, it was pretty much the same setting with some flavor text differences.<br /> <br /> I was expecting something that would be explicit about the differences from the game in the modern setting and the same one. What exactly does a Prince do if things like domain, progeny, accounting, and destruction is all in the hands of the sire or eldest of the line? What percentage of childer should be blood bonded to their sire compared to the norm in the modern age? What other things that would be illegal or frowned upon in the modern Camarilla as abusive are the norm in the Dark Ages? How are things different for vampires in the Age of Faith than the modern Age in terms of things like humanity and True Faith? Should vampires really be an urban predator when most &quot;cities&quot; are only 10,000 or so, with very few larger cities? Shouldn't we be seeing more Castle Dracula style havens with a small number of vampires in a more rural setting? Shouldn't the center of vampire &quot;civilization&quot; be the Islamic world with its older and larger cities than the relatively backward Europe? How does this impact the vampire rivalry with the Lupines? How can they avoid detection or attention or dissuade attacks on them? There was opportunity here to see two different kind of vampire domains - the relatively few urban areas that could house a lot of vampires, and the more typical rural based vampires hiding out in ruins and castles or tombs.<br /> <br /> I really wanted to see just how radical the Anarch Revolt and the establishment of the Camarilla had been.<br /> <br /> We never got it. Instead it was mostly more of the same which lead to lot of retroactive continuity changes. Instead, it was just more of the same but with all clans present. They even brought back the sect wars with an equivalent in the War of Princes though by then I had stopped buying the line.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Elphilm</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1515091-drama-or-realism?p=1515447#post1515447\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm not sure if it's possible to construct a playable setting out of that premise, but it's interesting to think about.\u200b</div> </div> </div> </div>That's probably the major reason. While I think it's possible to do an RPG game about WoD style vampires in a medieval setting, it'd be VERY different than one set in the modern nights. And that would subvert too many expectations of fans (especially that one can pick any clan they want and expect to work with equally random PCs), and require the writers to come up with all new ideas to accommodate play. I think perhaps the original developers and creators might have done it since they had given thought to what existed beforehand. But the people who came afterwards, no matter their individual strengths, were all in the same like of developing the existing work, and thus derivative. (Strangely we did get to see some creativity later in the second WoD/CoD by many of those same people; it would have been nicer to see something along those lines just to make things different.)<br /> <br /> I do think there is a lot to like in Dark Ages, but in many ways it presented the first Earth-Two in a WoD multiverse where the different editions were really different game worlds, albeit using a lot of the same concepts.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-18T12:53:57-07:00","id":1515473,"title":"","content":"Part of the problem is that &quot;retcon ravioli&quot; is pervasive in the WoD setting.<br /> <br /> For instance, Rasputin was canonically stated as being a Malkavian, a Follower of Set, a Ventrue, or a Nosferatu. The other game lines also claimed him as being a Shadowlord werewolf, a Cultist of Ecstasy mage, and Puppeteer wraith. So having Cappadocious call a clan meeting to get around 12,000 in attendance and still have not everyone show up is less than iron clad. Don't get me wrong, having unreliable narrators and information distorted by retellings over time make for good story fodder, but it sucks for world building.<br /> <br /> Personally I do run with the section from the 3rd edition/revised Storytellers Handbook<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> &quot;How many vampires are there, anyway?<br /> <br /> From a strictly Cainite point of view, probably around 40,000. The Middle Kingdom has roughly 20,000 Kuei-jin, for a grand total of approximately 60,000 \u2013 one for every 100,000 people on the planet.<br /> <br /> Of those Kindred, the clans make up the enormous majority of the population, leaving the rare bloodlines, fallen clans and other, less-quantifiable vampires at about 2,000 vampires in total. Cities aren't likely to have more than one or two Harbingers of Skulls or Gargoyles, if they have any at all. In fact, most cities don't have any Kindred other than those of the clans \u2013 and that's precisely how the clans like it. It is, after all, better to deal with the devil you know.\u2026\u200b&quot; </div> </div> </div>Assuming 13 clans divided into 38,000 vampires, that only works out to less than 3,000 per clan, with the remaining bloodlines being the aforementioned 2,000 vampires. But here is the kicker, that is using relatively modern population ratios.<br /> <br /> In 1800 the world population was estimated to be about 1 Billion mortals, which would mean a vampire population of 10,000 total (cainites &amp; kuei-jin). That would in turn mean that roughly each of the 13 clans only had roughly about 500 members and that covered the ones awake, in torpor, in different planes of existence. Now divide that number down by how many cities were over 100,000 and the number of places with vampires drops to a handful of locations.<br /> <br /> With those sorts of numbers, a single coterie ran a city state or a small kingdom or country with the only conflict coming from a rival coterie or other supernaturals. But once again that is only using 1800s era population calculations, so good luck applying the ratios to the world back in the 1500s or the actual Dark Ages.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-18T14:42:39-07:00","id":1515481,"title":"","content":"When it comes to vampire populations throughout history, I tend to follow this kind of line of thought;<br /> <br /> In Antiquity the local vampire club membership might not even register in double digits; outside of &quot;metropolitan&quot; areas, that's a population that can be spread across (what will one day be) entire countries. Ghouls take the place of neonates (with your domitors being, like, 8th generation or lower, and the 4th generation being active, disciplines past the first dot are <i>way</i> more common).<br /> Most embraces take place when a vampire decides to go into torpor and needs somebody to look after their stuff for a few centuries while they're gone (with a clear warning that they'll be back for it one night)<br /> <br /> - The vamps are claiming tribes and notable families/institutions and playing long distance strategy games with them, much like they will later. The toys and the rules may adapt, but the spirit of the game stays the same.<br /> - Respecting and obeying your elders is way more important when they're what's protecting you when the Garou are nowhere near critically endangered.<br /> - In many places the other clans are considered a myth or are totally unheard of.<br /> <br /> The numbers of vampires in an area often fluctuates hard for much of human history. Club membership increases with population growth (allowing siring more frequently) and immigration from foreign childer looking to strike out from under their sire's thumbs accompanying human invasions and migration periods (which slowly introduces the 13 clans into every area over the centuries). It also decreases when the herd population crashes during times of plagues and war, which may wipe entire tribes and permanent settlements off the map.<br /> <br /> The &quot;modern&quot; system starts shaping in the Medieval period, when stuff like that newfangled feudal system comes into fashion. London has about 6-10 vamps. Paris had around 20 whole vampires at one point. Cairo can support somewhere just over 30. Rural areas are &quot;family&quot; affairs, with one elder plus a childe or two and a few ghouls claiming domain over a town, village, fiefdom, whatever. It's pretty much 1 domain = 1 coterie.<br /> Meanwhile, the Inquisition does a damn good job of almost driving vampires totally extinct.<br /> <br /> So far, embraces have been uncommon enough that generations above 10th haven't been seen yet (at least not enough to be common knowledge).<br /> <br /> The Industrial Revolution sees rapid population growth amongst the kine, allowing for vamp population growth. There are enough vampires to form rival coteries within the same city. Generation 11+ start to show themselves, introducing the first generation of thinbloods (in the modern nights, generations 11-13 are shocked to learn that the Elders file them under this umbrella). World Wars start putting in a few dents, as well as clearing space for new blood in places.<br /> <br /> Growth continues. By the time we've reached the 21st century vampire populations can be sustained in the triple digits. No longer is the undead club a tight knit community where everyone knows everybody else. The kids have forgotten that 10th generation is the cutoff for a &quot;real&quot; vampire; the reasons the Traditions were codified seem out of touch; they don't feel threatened with the notion that human hunters can successfully wipe them *all* from the earth; they aren't convinced that &quot;filial piety&quot; towards the Elders is required, because are werewolves even real? 9 times out of 10, if you go out into the woods you won't even see one, and those that do don't live to spread the news.<br /> <br /> The Elders have spent centuries in a totally different environment before the world suddenly sprung this set up on them and are just a bit irritated. Jesus Christ, there are childer everywhere, when did their domains turn into playgrounds full of entitled screeching brats??\u200b","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><sup><i>Prone to hyperbole</i></sup>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26836-rhywbeth","name":"Rhywbeth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26836_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"377"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-18T14:46:21-07:00","id":1515482,"title":"","content":"* An addendum; The neonates are not <i>all </i>&quot;entitled screeching brats,&quot; many sires know better, but it's still annoying having other people's kids taking up space all over the place all of a sudden.<br /> (I'm not risking editing that into my post)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><sup><i>Prone to hyperbole</i></sup>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26836-rhywbeth","name":"Rhywbeth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26836_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"377"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-18T22:49:31-07:00","id":1515514,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1515091-drama-or-realism?p=1515473#post1515473\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">For instance, Rasputin was canonically stated as being a Malkavian, a Follower of Set, a Ventrue, or a Nosferatu. The other game lines also claimed him as being a Shadowlord werewolf, a Cultist of Ecstasy mage, and Puppeteer wraith.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, this is less retcon than an intentional display by the creators to inform the readers that IC claims should be taken with a grain of salt. People were not retconning each other. It's more of a nod and a wink to the readers. If you read the history of the Nosferatu Rasputin in Berlin By Night, you can easily make out this is someone pretending to claim he is Rasputin, not the actual one. Still, I admit it can be confusing until you figure out what is meant.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-18T22:55:14-07:00","id":1515516,"title":"","content":"For Rasputin Beckett's Jyhad Diary's &quot;explanation&quot;/possibility was quite nice<br /> Simply, there are many Rasputins, they are some sort of an anomaly, alternate selves but not in alternate dimensions, all of them are &quot;here&quot; and they are constantly in search of their &quot;Brothers&quot;.<br /> Actually was a clever move to solve that gordian knot","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","name":"Shadeprowler","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23890_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"928"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-19T09:41:21-07:00","id":1515543,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1515091-drama-or-realism?p=1515097#post1515097\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">When given the choice between a special forces soldier, a top tier scientist, a fortune 500 company owner, or a random highschooler who lacks experience in anything, why would you ever choose the highschooler except for contrived story reasons or for player options?</div> </div> </div> </div>Masquerade issues with embracing high-profile mortals aside...<br /> <br /> Success in life doesn't necessarily equate to success in unlife, especially when clan, clan culture, and the needs of the clan or city rear their ugly heads. The most obvious example of this is the Toreador. Yeah, they're <i>totally</i> down for Embracing the tortured artist type whose work heralds seismic cultural shifts in popular culture and high art alike...then they lose their creativity, get all angsty, and walk straight into broad daylight for whatever the fuck reason. Or, the media mogul, booking agent, or recording label exec, whose influence is problematic to exert as a vampire and at best measured in decades, who are probably massive arrogant, entitled douchebags likely to get merced.<br /> <br /> Or they can just chow down on the cast of Mean Girls. Cady can't cope and greets the sun, Regina smarts off to the Brujah primogen and ends up a chunky smear on the wall, Karen shacks up with a Malkavian and runs off to start a cult on the west coast...and fucking Gretchen somehow manages to survive, learn what's what, and just blood bonds the tortured artist, media mogul, booking agent, or recording label exec. Same level of cultural influence, better insulated, and hedged risk to extant power structures and the Masquerade at large.<br /> <br /> Even the Ventrue, who talk mad shit about only embracing the highest of the high, and spend decades grooming potential embraces and fledglings, aren't immune. They want ambitious go-getters who prove themselves by achieving much in life...but they <i>need</i> &quot;recruits&quot; who will swallow their ego, sit down, shut the fuck up, and do what they're told, and who won't rock the boat or immediately start trying to fuck over their elders. If a Ventrue had to pick a single character from <i>Better Call Saul</i> to embrace, it'd be Howard Hamlin, not Jimmy, Chuck, Kim, Gus, and certainly none of the Salamancas. Howard's the fully institutionalized one.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-19T10:30:43-07:00","id":1515544,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Theodrim</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1515091-drama-or-realism?p=1515543#post1515543\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If a Ventrue had to pick a single character from <i>Better Call Saul</i> to embrace, it'd be Howard Hamlin, not Jimmy, Chuck, Kim, Gus, and certainly none of the Salamancas. Howard's the fully institutionalized one.</div> </div> </div> </div>+1 for the unexpected (but totally appropriate) nod to <i>BCS!</i>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1190-elphilm","name":"Elphilm","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1190_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"763"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-20T05:19:49-07:00","id":1515647,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1515091-drama-or-realism?p=1515514#post1515514\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well, this is less retcon than an intentional display by the creators to inform the readers that IC claims should be taken with a grain of salt. People were not retconning each other. It's more of a nod and a wink to the readers. If you read the history of the Nosferatu Rasputin in Berlin By Night, you can easily make out this is someone pretending to claim he is Rasputin, not the actual one. Still, I admit it can be confusing until you figure out what is meant.</div> </div> </div> </div>I guess it is a matter of perspective. If you are looking back over the entire run of lore, it looks like there was a plan to leave things loose and undefined to allow for different stories to be told and to make sure players couldn't just read a book and automatically know what every character was.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, if you were around when the books were first coming out, it felt like every release showed a lack of consistency in the writing and that no one was checking the writing bible for the game line. It wouldn't exactly be difficult or revolutionary to just make sure all historical characters were never stated to be any type of supernatural.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-20T05:38:02-07:00","id":1515649,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Theodrim</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1515091-drama-or-realism?p=1515543#post1515543\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Masquerade issues with embracing high-profile mortals aside...<br /> <br /> Success in life doesn't necessarily equate to success in unlife, especially when clan, clan culture, and the needs of the clan or city rear their ugly heads. The most obvious example of this is the Toreador. Yeah, they're <i>totally</i> down for Embracing the tortured artist type whose work heralds seismic cultural shifts in popular culture and high art alike...then they lose their creativity, get all angsty, and walk straight into broad daylight for whatever the fuck reason. Or, the media mogul, booking agent, or recording label exec, whose influence is problematic to exert as a vampire and at best measured in decades, who are probably massive arrogant, entitled douchebags likely to get merced.</div> </div> </div> </div>And yet if you take a closer look into the individuals who IRL are those which own industries or at least guide them, they tend to be private people who go long periods of time without being seen. Once your business gets to a certain size the running of it has to be delegated to underlings, so the day to day stuff doesn't require the owner to be present. As for only getting a few decades out of the Owner before they would have to fake their death, it's not that difficult to change the leadership design to have a false front and allow the owner to run things secretly from the shadows, especially if you have years or decades to set things up.<br /> <br /> As far as success in unlife is concerned, what is required is the personal willingness to achieve, adapt, and overcome. These qualities are present in those individuals who built their empires during their mortal days. They were embraced by a given clan to lend their influence or power to said clan, as long as the sire protects the neonate long enough for them to adjust to the new rules of existence, then they will achieve, adapt, and overcome.<br /> <br /> A different way of looking at it is getting a pet. If you are lonely, then any kind of pet will do, heck I have seen people who treat potted plants with near pet like qualities. But if you are looking for a pet that can and will protect you, then you pretty much want a dog, and one that is of a larger variety. But there is nothing saying that a huge mastiff can't also keep you company. Vampire embraces work the same way, in that sure you might want a lover or friend, but if you are going to sink decades worth of training and resources into a childer then you will want something that gives additional benefits.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-20T07:00:25-07:00","id":1515660,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1515091-drama-or-realism?p=1515649#post1515649\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> And yet if you take a closer look into the individuals who IRL are those which own industries or at least guide them, they tend to be private people who go long periods of time without being seen. Once your business gets to a certain size the running of it has to be delegated to underlings, so the day to day stuff doesn't require the owner to be present. As for only getting a few decades out of the Owner before they would have to fake their death, it's not that difficult to change the leadership design to have a false front and allow the owner to run things secretly from the shadows, especially if you have years or decades to set things up.</div> </div> </div> </div>Sure, there are plenty of Howard Hughes types out there, particularly in industries of ill repute. But, we live in a world of celebrity CEO's, finance brokers and venture capitalists, &quot;activist&quot; investors, and corporate consultants. The expectation of a business, political, or cultural elite today is to make regular media appearances, be active on social media, and cross-pollinate ideas on lecture, symposium, talking head, and keynote circuits. And, the biggest cottage industry common to all these high-profile sectors is image management and reputation laundering, to make elites appear as healthy, well-balanced, stable human beings opposed to high-functioning (at best), entitled, sociopaths...or failing that in extreme examples (ex. Steve Jobs), idealize their cartoonish despicable behavior.<br /> <br /> That can only go so far. Deepfake technology hasn't gotten <i>quite</i> that good yet.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> As far as success in unlife is concerned, what is required is the personal willingness to achieve, adapt, and overcome. These qualities are present in those individuals who built their empires during their mortal days. </div> </div> </div>Not necessarily, least of all in the updated 5th edition setting where neonates and fledglings will inevitably be gen-X'ers, millennials, and zoomers. The generations there aren't the issue per se, but rather the culture in which those generations live: dominated by the financial sector, mass media, internet and social media, and tech industry. &quot;Achieve, adapt, and overcome&quot; is <i>far</i> from the recipe for success in contemporary hyperreality.<br /> <br /> I'd go so far as to say the traits that make someone successful as mortals in today's society, ultimately make them <i>worse</i> embrace candidates. All that mortal success and entitlement goes right out the window the nanosecond a neonate gets stuck in a room with a zero-fuck-giving ancilla or elder who has the prestation credit and status to simply end their unlife, and probably get a written apology and prestation boon out of it from their sire for failing to make a prudent embrace and teach their childe right.<br /> <br /> Acting like a complete shithead to other vampires without regard for the consequences is for those have enough Fortitude to survive a dragon's breath load to the face. It didn't work for Hardestadt, and it definitely wouldn't work for a neonatal Ventrue tech bro who absolutely would not be able to keep their ego in check in an Elysium.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":431,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","user":"Theodrim","date":". 06-20-2023, 10:04 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-21T11:56:22-07:00","id":1515767,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Theodrim</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1515091-drama-or-realism?p=1515660#post1515660\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Sure, there are plenty of Howard Hughes types out there, particularly in industries of ill repute. But, we live in a world of celebrity CEO's, finance brokers and venture capitalists, &quot;activist&quot; investors, and corporate consultants. The expectation of a business, political, or cultural elite today is to make regular media appearances, be active on social media, and cross-pollinate ideas on lecture, symposium, talking head, and keynote circuits. And, the biggest cottage industry common to all these high-profile sectors is image management and reputation laundering, to make elites appear as healthy, well-balanced, stable human beings opposed to high-functioning (at best), entitled, sociopaths...or failing that in extreme examples (ex. Steve Jobs), idealize their cartoonish despicable behavior.<br /> <br /> That can only go so far. Deepfake technology hasn't gotten <i>quite</i> that good yet.</div> </div> </div> </div>It is not a question of social expectations, but rather the fact that you can set things up years in advance, transfer all the control to a shadowy holding company, and then arrange for your &quot;death&quot; in a minor plane crash in the wilderness, a small yacht sinking with all hands lost, etc. Yes there will be a little bit of news cycle focus for a few months, but then you just hang out in a 3rd world country for a decade or so to directly oversee your holdings there. By the time you come back to your old stomping grounds no one should be able to recognize you.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Theodrim</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1515091-drama-or-realism?p=1515660#post1515660\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Not necessarily, least of all in the updated 5th edition setting where neonates and fledglings will inevitably be gen-X'ers, millennials, and zoomers. The generations there aren't the issue per se, but rather the culture in which those generations live: dominated by the financial sector, mass media, internet and social media, and tech industry. &quot;Achieve, adapt, and overcome&quot; is <i>far</i> from the recipe for success in contemporary hyperreality.<br /> <br /> I'd go so far as to say the traits that make someone successful as mortals in today's society, ultimately make them <i>worse</i> embrace candidates. All that mortal success and entitlement goes right out the window the nanosecond a neonate gets stuck in a room with a zero-fuck-giving ancilla or elder who has the prestation credit and status to simply end their unlife, and probably get a written apology and prestation boon out of it from their sire for failing to make a prudent embrace and teach their childe right.</div> </div> </div> </div>The outlook you are espousing is of the modern rabble who would never be considered for the embrace because they have nothing to offer. There are uncommon individuals who achieve, adapt, and overcome. This is turn is part of what catches the potential sires eye.<br /> <br /> VtM was always designed with two contradictory ideas in mind. The first is the Lost Boys group of vampires who are on a very street gang level of existence. They party every night, they do stupid adrenaline junkie things for fun, all the powers of youth and none of the responsibilities. This appeals to the teenage demographic who yearns for such things and due to their limited life experience, think that such things are the only way to exist.<br /> <br /> But at the same time, the setting talks about elders this and elders that, they control everything, they have influence everywhere, they shape whole societies to their whim. These make excellent bad guys for the PC teenagers to rage against. But their very existence means there is another value set for vampires which prizes competency and control over pointless hedonism. But unlike biological creatures with a life cycle, vampires are turned fully formed, meaning the recently turned banker will enjoy the perks of unlife, but will still be financially educated and detail oriented.<br /> <br /> One could make the argument that those selected by elders or just mature neonates for the embrace are examples of artificial selection, while the people embraced by the Lost Boys types are more akin to social mutation. Both methods cause shifts to the overall vampire population makeup, but the former tend to survive longer and are able to network better within their own clans, which means the overall vampiric population should be leaning more in that direction.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-21T16:42:48-07:00","id":1515800,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1515091-drama-or-realism?p=1515767#post1515767\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> The outlook you are espousing is of the modern rabble who would never be considered for the embrace...the Lost Boys group of vampires who are on a very street gang level of existence. They party every night, they do stupid adrenaline junkie things for fun, all the powers of youth and none of the responsibilities.</div> </div> </div> </div>Your implication is political, social, economic, and cultural elites <i>don't</i> act like this?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-21T18:19:53-07:00","id":1515812,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Theodrim</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1515091-drama-or-realism?p=1515800#post1515800\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Your implication is political, social, economic, and cultural elites <i>don't</i> act like this?</div> </div> </div> </div>My implication is that the original creators of that power level will have the skills &amp; mindset in addition to the tendency to overindulge. The following generations who were born to luxury and power rarely develop the skills and mindset. Hence the concept that &quot;the first generation builds the empire, the second generation maintains it, and the third generation loses it&quot;.<br /> <br /> But those that never build anything, never achieve anything, never create anything, why in the world would they be considered for the embrace? If they are pretty or need to be used as a catspaw, just ghoul them. Since they don't age, they have all of eternity to earn the embrace or fail and die. Regardless if you ghoul them or embrace them, once they stop aging, you only have about 10 years before you need to change their location which brings us right back to the notion of faking ones death or changing the paperwork regarding who controls what.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}